readings.c2s1:
¶

'''unhoard''' (un-hōrd′), ''v. t.'' To dissipate; scatter. ''Milton'', P. L., iv. 188. [Rare.]

'''unhold'''<sup>1</sup>† (un-hōld′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''hold''<sup>1</sup>.] To cease to hold; let go the hold of. ''Otway.''

'''unhold'''<sup>2</sup>†, ''a.'' [< ME. ''unhold'', < AS. ''unhold'' (= OS. OHG. ''unhold''), < ''un-'', not, + ''hold'', faithful: see ''hold''<sup>2</sup>.] Unfavorable; hostile.

'''unhole'''†, ''a.'' A Middle English form of ''unwhole.''

'''unholily''' (un-hō′li-li), ''adv.'' In an unholy manner. ''Jer. Taylor'', Rule of Conscience, ii. 3.

'''unholiness''' (un-hō′li-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being unholy; want of holiness.
{{smaller block|The ''unholiness'' of obtruding upon men remission of sins for money.
{{right|''Raleigh.''}}}}

'''unholsom'''†, ''a.'' A Middle English form of ''unwholesome.''

'''unholy''' (un-hō′li), ''a.'' and ''n.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' Not holy. {{smaller|(''a'') Not sacred; not hallowed or consecrated.}}
{{smaller block|Doth it follow that all things now in the church are ''unholy'' which the Lord himself hath not precisely instituted?
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Impious; wicked.}}
{{smaller block|Blasphemers, disobedient to parents, unthankful, ''unholy.''
{{right|2 Tim. iii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' (''a'') Unhallowed, unsanctified. (''b'') Profane, ungodly.}}

'''II.''' ''n.''; pl. ''unholies'' (-liz). That which is unholy. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|How many other ''Unholies'' has your covering Art made holy, besides this Arabian Whinstone.
{{right|''Carlyle'', Sartor Resartus.}}}}

'''unhomogeneous''' (un-hō-mọ̄-jē′nẹ̄-us), ''a.'' Not homogeneous; heterogeneous.

'''unhomogeneousness''' (un-hō-mọ̄-jē′nẹ̄-us-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being unhomo-

readings.c2s2:
geneous; heterogeneousness.

'''unhonest†''' (un-on′est), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unhonest;'' < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''honest.''] Dishonest; dishonorable; not virtuous; unchaste.
{{smaller block|Whenne yee er sette, take noone ''vnhoneste'' tale.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Then, lady, you must know, you are held ''unhonest;''
The Duke, your brother, and your friends in court,
With too much grief condemn you.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Woman-Hater, v. 5.}}}}

'''unhonestly†''' (un-on′est-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''unhonestly;'' < ''unhonest'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Dishonestly; improperly; unchastely.
{{smaller block|Speke neuer ''vnhonestly'' of woman kynde.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 306.}}}}

'''unhonesty†''' (un-on′es-ti), ''n.'' Dishonesty; impropriety; improper conduct.
{{smaller block|''Unhonesty'' hath ever present pleasure in it, having neither good pretence going before, nor yet any profit following after. {{right|''Ascham'', Toxophilus (ed. 1864), p. 39.}}}}

'''unhonor†, unhonour†''' (un-on′o̤r), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''unhonouren;'' < ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''honor.''] To dishonor.
{{smaller block|I honoure my Fadir, and ye han ''unhonourid'' me.
{{right|''Wyclif'', John viii.}}}}

'''unhonored, unhonoured''' (un-on′o̤rd), ''a.'' Not honored; not regarded with honor or veneration.
{{smaller block|Unwept, ''unhonour’d'', and unsung.
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., vi. 1.}}}}

'''unhooded''' (un-hu̇d′ed), ''a.'' Not having or not covered with a hood.
{{smaller block|Up soars one falcon ''unhooded'', while the other is drawn from its uncertain perch on the head of the Arab to join the others. {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVII. 82.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''unhook''' (un-hu̇k′), ''v. t.'' To loose from a hook; open or undo by detaching the hook or hooks of.

'''unhoop''' (un-höp′), ''v. t.'' '''1.''' To remove the hoops of, as a barrel or cask.—'''2.''' To remove the stiff petticoats or hoop-skirts of, as a woman: probably jocose, and with allusion to def. 1.
{{smaller block|''Unhoop'' the fair sex, and cure this fashionable tympany got among them.
{{right|''Addison.''}}}}

'''unhoped''' (un-hōpt′), ''a.'' Not hoped or looked for; unexpected; not so probable as to excite hope.
{{smaller block|Whatsoevere thou mayst sen that is don in this world ''unhoped'' or unwenyd.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, iv. prose 6.}}
With ''unhop’d'' success.
{{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, vii. 400.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Unhoped-for''', unhoped; not hoped for.}}

'''unhopeful''' (un-hōp′fu̇l), ''a.'' Not hopeful; leaving no room for hope; hopeless. ''Shak.'', Much Ado, ii. 1. 392.

'''unhopefully''' (un-hōp′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' In an unhopeful manner; without hope; hopelessly. ''Fortnightly Rev.'', N. S., XLI. 833.

'''unhorse''' (un-hôrs′), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''unhorsen'', ''onhorsen''; < ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''horse''.] '''1.''' To throw or strike down from a horse; cause to dismount or fall from the saddle.
{{smaller block|But thei were clene ''onhorsid'' in the feld.
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2464.}}
He would ''unhorse'' the lustiest challenger.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., v. 3. 19.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|''Unhuman'' and remorseless cruelty.
{{right|''South'', Sermons, XI. ii.}}}}

'''unhumanize''' (un-hū′mạn-iz), ''v. t.'' [< ''unhuman'' + ''-ize.''] To cause to cease to be human; deprive or divest of the nature or characteristics of human beings. ''Ruskin.''

'''unhung''' (un-hung′), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not suspended; not hung.—'''2.''' Not hanged; unhanged.

'''unhurt''' (un-hėrt′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unhurt''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''hurt.''] Not hurt; not harmed; free from injury.
{{smaller block|That y<sup>e</sup> Mayre and citezens haue alle their liberties and free vsage ''vnhurt.'' {{right|''Arnold’s Chron.'', p. 2.}}

Through burning climes I passed ''unhurt.''
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 489.}}}}

'''unhurtful''' (un-hėrt′fu̇l), ''a.'' Not hurtful; wanting the power of doing harm or injury. ''Shak.'', M. for M., iii. 2. 175.

'''unhurtfully''' (un-hėrt′fu̇l-li), ''adv.'' Without harm; harmlessly. ''Pope.''

'''unhurtfulness''' (un-hėrt′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' Harmlessness.
{{smaller block|Your ''unhurtefulnes'' shall condemne theyr unclennes.
{{right|''Udall'', 1 Cor. vi. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

'''unhusbanded''' (un-huz′bạn-ded), ''a.'' '''1.''' Having no husband; unmarried; also, deprived of a husband; widowed.
{{smaller block|<poem>With hanging head I have beheld
A widow vine stand in a naked field,
''Unhusbanded'', neglected, all forlorne.</poem>
{{right|''Browne'', Britannia’s Pastorals, ii. 5.}}}}
'''2.''' Not managed with care or frugality; uncultivated.
{{smaller block|The plains about are well-nigh overgrown with bushes and ''unhusbanded.'' {{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 110.}}}}
